[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Optimistic Locking with @Version in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Data concurrency is a critical challenge in enterprise applications. When multiple transactions attempt to update the same record simultaneously, standard database operations can cause lost updates or dirty reads. Hibernate provides a built-in mechanism to handle this gracefully using Optimistic Locking via the @Version annotation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Optimistic Locking?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Optimistic locking assumes that multiple transactions can complete without affecting each other, and therefore transactions can proceed without locking data resources upfront. Before committing, each transaction verifies that no other transaction has modified the data it read. If a conflicting modification is detected, the transaction throws an OptimisticLockException and rolls back."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation using @Version"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To enable optimistic locking in your Hibernate entity, simply add a version property annotated with @Version. Hibernate supports numeric types (such as int, Integer, long, Long) as well as timestamp types for the version field."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.Version;
import jakarta.persistence.Table;

@Entity
@Table(name = "products")
public class Product {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    private Double price;

    @Version
    private Long version;

    // Getters and Setters
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"How it Works Behind the Scenes"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When the Product entity is first loaded, Hibernate reads the current version value (e.g., version = 1)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When an update is flushed to the database, Hibernate generates an UPDATE statement that includes a check against the version column."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Example generated SQL: UPDATE products SET name = ?, price = ?, version = 2 WHERE id = 1 AND version = 1."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If another transaction updated the row in the meantime, the version in the database will be 2, meaning the WHERE clause will match 0 rows. Hibernate immediately detects this and throws an OptimisticLockException."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling OptimisticLockException"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When an OptimisticLockException occurs, the application should handle the failure gracefully. Typically, this involves notifying the user that the data was modified by another user, refreshing the entity with the latest database state, or retrying the operation."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"try {
    product.setPrice(99.99);
    entityManager.merge(product);
    entityManager.getTransaction().commit();
} catch (OptimisticLockException e) {
    // Handle conflict: roll back, notify user, or retry
    entityManager.getTransaction().rollback();
    System.out.println("Conflict detected! Another user updated this product.");
}"}]
